WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:05.918
350 metres down, a drilling
rig has lost its position.

2
00:00:05.958 --> 00:00:09.448
The riser drags the
blowout preventer sideways,

3
00:00:09.578 --> 00:00:14.288
and the wellhead below it takes
more than 12 MNm of bending,

4
00:00:14.328 --> 00:00:16.214
on top of the well pressure.

5
00:00:16.254 --> 00:00:20.654
The engineer opens the finite element
model of the wellhead housing,

6
00:00:20.754 --> 00:00:22.362
and there it is.

7
00:00:22.742 --> 00:00:24.193
A red spot.

8
00:00:24.233 --> 00:00:27.433
793 MPa.

9
00:00:27.473 --> 00:00:30.136
50 % above the yield strength.

10
00:00:31.083 --> 00:00:32.920
So, has it failed?

11
00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:37.380
Is the acceptance criterion the
yield strength of the material?

12
00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:42.714
Or is it a strain limit, from
an elastic plastic analysis?

13
00:00:42.754 --> 00:00:48.813
And what exactly should we take out of
the FE model, and compare with what?

14
00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:57.240
In this video we answer those questions
the way NORSOK U-001, the 2026 edition,

15
00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:59.158
does it in its Annex B.

16
00:00:59.198 --> 00:01:04.618
We will read the stress strain diagram,
extract stresses from an FE model,

17
00:01:04.658 --> 00:01:10.421
run the elastic plastic check, and at
the end, give the red spot its verdict.

18
00:01:14.583 --> 00:01:17.296
First, which rules apply to what.

19
00:01:17.336 --> 00:01:19.189
Annex B is normative.

20
00:01:19.229 --> 00:01:23.937
It covers the pressure containing and
primary structural components in the riser

21
00:01:23.977 --> 00:01:27.579
load path: the wellhead
housing and its extension,

22
00:01:27.619 --> 00:01:31.905
the conductor housing and its
extension, the connectors, the tree,

23
00:01:31.945 --> 00:01:34.648
and the workover riser equipment.

24
00:01:34.688 --> 00:01:37.438
Straight pipe, like the conductor itself,

25
00:01:37.478 --> 00:01:40.969
has its own capacity
formulas in clause B.9.

26
00:01:41.249 --> 00:01:46.985
And the template is designed to
NORSOK N-001, outside Annex B.

27
00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:53.123
Annex B asks us to design against
failure modes, not against a stress.

28
00:01:53.163 --> 00:01:57.843
The structural ones are brittle
fracture, yielding, local failure,

29
00:01:57.883 --> 00:02:00.489
buckling and mechanical disengagement.

30
00:02:00.529 --> 00:02:04.629
The functional ones include hub
face separation, leak tightness,

31
00:02:04.669 --> 00:02:06.726
galling and loss of function.

32
00:02:06.766 --> 00:02:11.106
Brittle fracture and environmental
cracking are prevented by the material

33
00:02:11.146 --> 00:02:14.183
requirements, not by analysis.

34
00:02:14.463 --> 00:02:17.339
And the static capacity
of a component is the

35
00:02:17.379 --> 00:02:20.775
lowest capacity over
all the remaining modes.

36
00:02:21.717 --> 00:02:25.046
Then comes the design format, formula B.2.

37
00:02:25.086 --> 00:02:30.766
The load, or load effect, shall not
exceed the static design factor, Fd,

38
00:02:30.806 --> 00:02:32.703
times the static capacity.

39
00:02:32.743 --> 00:02:35.765
Fd comes from Table B.3.

40
00:02:36.045 --> 00:02:41.765
0.67 for normal conditions,
0.8 for extreme,

41
00:02:41.805 --> 00:02:46.419
and 0.9 or 1.0 for accidental conditions.

42
00:02:46.459 --> 00:02:54.223
Pressure design on its own uses 0.6, and
functional failure modes always use 1.0.

43
00:02:54.263 --> 00:02:57.620
Now look at both sides of that inequality.

44
00:02:57.900 --> 00:02:59.353
Both are loads.

45
00:02:59.393 --> 00:03:04.098
The acceptance criterion is
not a stress, and not a strain.

46
00:03:04.138 --> 00:03:07.621
It is a load, compared with a capacity.

47
00:03:08.583 --> 00:03:12.764
So the real question is:
how do we find the capacity?

48
00:03:12.804 --> 00:03:16.357
Clause B.3.3 gives three ways.

49
00:03:16.397 --> 00:03:20.260
By formulas, for simple
shapes like pipes and flanges.

50
00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:23.400
For pipe, the yield
strength enters directly:

51
00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:27.090
the fully plastic section
for seamless riser pipe,

52
00:03:27.130 --> 00:03:31.006
and first yield at the outer
fibre for conductors and casing.

53
00:03:31.046 --> 00:03:34.576
Second, by elastic
finite element analysis,

54
00:03:34.616 --> 00:03:37.507
which NORSOK limits to preliminary design.

55
00:03:37.547 --> 00:03:42.954
And third, by elastic plastic finite
element analysis, the direct method.

56
00:03:42.994 --> 00:03:48.354
The yield strength enters all three,
but in a different way each time,

57
00:03:48.394 --> 00:03:51.726
and strain only appears in the last one.

58
00:03:52.006 --> 00:03:55.876
To see why, we need the
stress strain diagram.

59
00:04:00.017 --> 00:04:02.655
Everything starts with a tensile test.

60
00:04:02.695 --> 00:04:07.536
A specimen is pulled, and we plot the
force divided by the original area,

61
00:04:07.576 --> 00:04:12.051
against the elongation divided
by the original length.

62
00:04:12.091 --> 00:04:14.277
First the line is straight.

63
00:04:14.557 --> 00:04:20.236
Stress is proportional to strain, the
slope is Young's modulus, and if we unload,

64
00:04:20.276 --> 00:04:22.141
the specimen springs back.

65
00:04:22.181 --> 00:04:25.912
Then the line bends: the
steel starts to yield.

66
00:04:25.952 --> 00:04:29.896
It hardens, up to a maximum,
the tensile strength.

67
00:04:29.936 --> 00:04:33.785
After that the specimen
necks, and finally it breaks.

68
00:04:34.750 --> 00:04:36.988
Where exactly does yielding start?

69
00:04:37.028 --> 00:04:40.552
There is no sharp point,
so we agree on one.

70
00:04:40.592 --> 00:04:46.521
Draw a line parallel to the elastic
line, starting at 0.2 % strain.

71
00:04:46.561 --> 00:04:49.988
Where it crosses the curve
is the yield strength.

72
00:04:50.028 --> 00:04:55.693
Unload from there, and the specimen
keeps 0.2 % of permanent strain.

73
00:04:55.733 --> 00:04:58.100
NORSOK uses exactly this.

74
00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:03.000
The yield strength is the
stress at a 0.2 % offset,

75
00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:06.819
and the failure mode called
yielding is defined as exceeding

76
00:05:06.859 --> 00:05:11.810
that elastic limit of
0.2 % plastic strain.

77
00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:16.786
The design does not use
the result of one test.

78
00:05:16.826 --> 00:05:22.667
Formula B.5 takes the specified minimum
yield strength, de-rated for temperature,

79
00:05:22.707 --> 00:05:27.825
but never more than 90 % of the
de-rated minimum tensile strength.

80
00:05:27.865 --> 00:05:30.976
Formula B.6 gives the tensile strength.

81
00:05:31.016 --> 00:05:34.450
De-rating is needed above 40 °C.

82
00:05:34.490 --> 00:05:43.150
Our example is a wellhead housing forged
in 75K material: 517 MPa minimum yield,

83
00:05:43.190 --> 00:05:46.473
and 655 tensile.

84
00:05:46.753 --> 00:05:54.306
Below 40 degrees, σy
is 517, and σu is 655.

85
00:05:55.250 --> 00:05:56.738
One more step.

86
00:05:56.778 --> 00:06:02.150
The engineering curve divides by the
original area, but the specimen gets thinner.

87
00:06:02.190 --> 00:06:08.540
Divide by the actual area and you get the
true stress, sigma times one plus epsilon,

88
00:06:08.580 --> 00:06:13.136
against the true strain, the
natural log of one plus epsilon.

89
00:06:13.176 --> 00:06:15.593
The true curve keeps rising.

90
00:06:15.633 --> 00:06:22.084
For our steel it reaches
743 MPa at 13 % true strain.

91
00:06:22.124 --> 00:06:25.675
FE programs with large
strains need this true curve,

92
00:06:25.715 --> 00:06:28.524
and if no test curve is available,

93
00:06:28.564 --> 00:06:35.710
NORSOK points to the material model in
ASME Section VIII, Division 3, KM-620.

94
00:06:36.650 --> 00:06:42.397
From the test curve, NORSOK builds three
material models for the FE analysis.

95
00:06:42.437 --> 00:06:45.519
A straight line, for the elastic analysis.

96
00:06:45.559 --> 00:06:50.129
The true curve, cut off at the
yield strength with no hardening,

97
00:06:50.169 --> 00:06:52.121
for the limit load method.

98
00:06:52.161 --> 00:06:59.831
A tiny slope is allowed to help the solver:
no more than 5 % at 20 % plastic strain.

99
00:06:59.871 --> 00:07:03.901
And the true curve cut off at
the true ultimate strength,

100
00:07:04.121 --> 00:07:09.017
for the plastic collapse method
and for the local failure check.

101
00:07:09.057 --> 00:07:11.728
Keep these three curves in mind.

102
00:07:12.008 --> 00:07:15.592
Each one belongs to a
different acceptance check.

103
00:07:19.750 --> 00:07:23.060
Here is our wellhead housing, cut in half.

104
00:07:23.100 --> 00:07:26.926
The FE model divides
it into small elements.

105
00:07:26.966 --> 00:07:31.846
At the wellhead datum we apply the
accidental loads from Table A.4,

106
00:07:31.906 --> 00:07:34.561
plus the rated working pressure inside.

107
00:07:34.601 --> 00:07:39.623
A linear elastic analysis gives six
stress components at every point.

108
00:07:39.663 --> 00:07:45.378
To plot them, we combine them into one
number: the von Mises equivalent stress.

109
00:07:46.317 --> 00:07:51.351
The von Mises stress takes the three
principal stresses and measures how much they

110
00:07:51.391 --> 00:07:54.612
distort the material,
rather than squeeze it.

111
00:07:54.652 --> 00:07:59.935
When it reaches the yield strength
from the tensile test, yielding starts.

112
00:07:59.975 --> 00:08:04.566
NORSOK allows von Mises or
Tresca for elastic analysis.

113
00:08:05.517 --> 00:08:07.434
Now, the red spot.

114
00:08:07.474 --> 00:08:14.604
793 MPa sits in a thin skin at the
fillet, about a centimetre deep.

115
00:08:14.644 --> 00:08:17.540
If that skin yields, nothing collapses.

116
00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:21.153
The load just moves to
the material next to it.

117
00:08:21.193 --> 00:08:27.277
A peak stress like this matters for
fatigue, because that is where cracks start.

118
00:08:27.557 --> 00:08:30.691
For static strength,
comparing the peak with

119
00:08:30.731 --> 00:08:33.913
the yield strength is the wrong question.

120
00:08:34.850 --> 00:08:39.510
Instead, we cut through the wall
with a stress classification line,

121
00:08:39.580 --> 00:08:43.451
from the bore to the outside,
at the critical section.

122
00:08:43.491 --> 00:08:46.671
Along that line we read
the stress components:

123
00:08:46.891 --> 00:08:49.238
the axial stress from bending and tension,

124
00:08:49.278 --> 00:08:52.954
and the hoop and radial
stresses from the pressure.

125
00:08:53.917 --> 00:08:59.852
Then we linearise, as described in ASME
Section VIII, Division 2, Annex 5-A.

126
00:08:59.892 --> 00:09:03.765
The membrane stress is
the average over the wall.

127
00:09:03.805 --> 00:09:08.361
The bending stress is the linear
part with the same moment.

128
00:09:08.401 --> 00:09:10.837
What is left over is the peak.

129
00:09:10.877 --> 00:09:16.118
Do that for every component, and
combine them with von Mises again.

130
00:09:16.398 --> 00:09:23.735
Here the membrane stress is 482
MPa, membrane plus bending is 613,

131
00:09:23.775 --> 00:09:30.233
and the peak adds about
180, to the total of 793.

132
00:09:31.183 --> 00:09:33.632
Each part gets a category.

133
00:09:33.672 --> 00:09:38.548
Primary stress is what the
component needs to carry the load.

134
00:09:38.588 --> 00:09:41.889
It does not go away when
the material yields.

135
00:09:41.929 --> 00:09:48.733
General membrane stress is Pm, local
membrane stress near a discontinuity is PL,

136
00:09:48.773 --> 00:09:50.254
and bending is Pb.

137
00:09:50.294 --> 00:09:56.324
Secondary stress, Q, comes from constraint,
like thermal expansion or preload,

138
00:09:56.364 --> 00:09:59.078
and it relaxes once the material yields.

139
00:09:59.118 --> 00:10:02.616
And the peak, F, only counts for fatigue.

140
00:10:02.656 --> 00:10:07.216
For preloaded equipment, if you cannot
separate primary from secondary,

141
00:10:07.256 --> 00:10:09.090
treat it all as primary.

142
00:10:10.050 --> 00:10:11.504
Now we compare.

143
00:10:11.544 --> 00:10:15.744
Table B.7 sets the membrane
stress against the yield strength,

144
00:10:15.914 --> 00:10:20.024
membrane plus bending against
1.5 times the yield strength,

145
00:10:20.184 --> 00:10:26.588
the primary plus secondary range against
two times, shear against 0.57 times,

146
00:10:26.628 --> 00:10:29.299
and bearing against the yield strength.

147
00:10:29.339 --> 00:10:31.658
Why 1.5 for bending?

148
00:10:31.938 --> 00:10:37.610
When the wall first yields at its
surface, the inside is still elastic.

149
00:10:37.650 --> 00:10:41.944
It can carry more, until the
whole thickness has yielded.

150
00:10:41.984 --> 00:10:45.654
For a rectangular wall,
that fully plastic moment

151
00:10:45.694 --> 00:10:49.513
is exactly 1.5 times
the first yield moment.

152
00:10:49.553 --> 00:10:56.377
And 0.57 is 1/√3, the shear
yield stress of von Mises.

153
00:10:57.317 --> 00:11:00.368
The design factor scales
the allowable stress.

154
00:11:00.408 --> 00:11:04.520
For the accidental load, Fd is 0.9.

155
00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:15.061
So the membrane stress of 482 must
stay below 0.9 times 517, which is 465.

156
00:11:15.101 --> 00:11:16.616
It does not.

157
00:11:16.656 --> 00:11:19.620
The elastic route fails, by 4 %.

158
00:11:19.660 --> 00:11:21.985
Membrane plus bending would pass.

159
00:11:22.025 --> 00:11:24.153
But read the fine print.

160
00:11:24.533 --> 00:11:28.823
NORSOK limits elastic analysis
to preliminary design,

161
00:11:28.953 --> 00:11:34.448
and for pressure containing parts, to
a diameter to thickness ratio above 10.

162
00:11:34.488 --> 00:11:37.213
Our neck has 6.25.

163
00:11:37.253 --> 00:11:41.368
So this result cannot be
used for acceptance at all.

164
00:11:41.408 --> 00:11:43.802
We need the direct method.

165
00:11:47.950 --> 00:11:52.395
An elastic plastic analysis
follows the material past yield.

166
00:11:52.435 --> 00:11:57.975
The FE model needs the von Mises
yield function with associated flow,

167
00:11:58.015 --> 00:12:02.555
large deformations and large
strains, and a hardening rule:

168
00:12:02.935 --> 00:12:08.765
isotropic for static capacities, kinematic
if the loading reverses beyond yield.

169
00:12:08.805 --> 00:12:14.252
The loads are applied in the real sequence:
preload, pressure and tension first.

170
00:12:14.532 --> 00:12:17.832
Then one load, here the bending moment,

171
00:12:17.882 --> 00:12:22.868
is increased step by step until
the component can take no more.

172
00:12:23.817 --> 00:12:29.164
First, the limit load method, with the
curve cut off at the yield strength.

173
00:12:29.204 --> 00:12:31.827
At first the neck is elastic.

174
00:12:31.867 --> 00:12:39.424
At close to 90 % of the final load, the
outer surface reaches 0.2 % plastic strain.

175
00:12:39.464 --> 00:12:40.948
It has yielded.

176
00:12:40.988 --> 00:12:45.648
With every step, the yielded zone
grows inwards from both sides,

177
00:12:45.728 --> 00:12:48.588
and the rotation grows
faster than the load.

178
00:12:48.628 --> 00:12:53.490
When the whole section has yielded,
it acts as a plastic hinge.

179
00:12:53.530 --> 00:12:58.707
The next load step finds no equilibrium,
and the solution does not converge.

180
00:12:58.747 --> 00:13:02.423
The last converged load is the limit load.

181
00:13:02.703 --> 00:13:06.049
For our neck, 20.3 MNm.

182
00:13:06.089 --> 00:13:10.669
That is the yielding capacity,
at a design factor of one.

183
00:13:11.617 --> 00:13:15.405
The second option is the
factored plastic collapse method.

184
00:13:15.445 --> 00:13:20.136
Now the material hardens, up
to the true ultimate strength.

185
00:13:20.176 --> 00:13:26.231
The moment climbs past the limit
load and peaks at 25.2 MNm.

186
00:13:26.271 --> 00:13:29.205
That maximum is the plastic collapse load.

187
00:13:29.245 --> 00:13:35.939
It is divided by the
larger of 1.2, and σu/σy.

188
00:13:35.979 --> 00:13:42.409
For our steel that is
1.27, which gives 19.9 MNm.

189
00:13:42.449 --> 00:13:44.460
Why 1.2?

190
00:13:44.739 --> 00:13:50.835
ASME Section VIII, Division 3, uses
a margin of 1.8 on plastic collapse,

191
00:13:50.875 --> 00:13:55.769
and the workover riser
standard uses 1.5 on yielding.

192
00:13:55.809 --> 00:14:03.101
1.8 over 1.5 is 1.2, so the
two methods are meant to agree.

193
00:14:03.141 --> 00:14:05.951
Here they do, within 2 %.

194
00:14:06.917 --> 00:14:11.604
For a connector, the same curve
carries more than one limit.

195
00:14:11.644 --> 00:14:16.906
Preload exceedance, seal leak tightness,
mechanical disengagement, the limit load,

196
00:14:16.946 --> 00:14:19.149
and the plastic collapse load.

197
00:14:19.189 --> 00:14:24.415
The static capacity is the lowest of the
structural and functional capacities.

198
00:14:24.455 --> 00:14:27.708
That is why NORSOK recommends
that you always plot

199
00:14:27.748 --> 00:14:31.487
the load against the
rotation or displacement.

200
00:14:32.450 --> 00:14:36.741
Single load capacities are
combined in a capacity chart.

201
00:14:36.781 --> 00:14:41.950
For linear behaviour, formula B.7 adds
the true wall tension over the tension

202
00:14:41.990 --> 00:14:49.023
capacity, and the moment over the bending
capacity, and the sum shall not exceed Fd.

203
00:14:49.063 --> 00:14:54.700
That gives one envelope per load
condition: normal, extreme, and accidental.

204
00:14:54.740 --> 00:14:58.470
Where pressure or tension
changes the bending capacity,

205
00:14:58.580 --> 00:15:05.380
NORSOK asks for nonlinear charts,
analysed with all the loads divided by Fd.

206
00:15:09.517 --> 00:15:12.900
So, is the acceptance
criterion a strain limit?

207
00:15:12.940 --> 00:15:15.124
For the yielding capacity, no.

208
00:15:15.164 --> 00:15:19.716
It is a load, and you do not
read off a strain to find it.

209
00:15:19.756 --> 00:15:23.920
Strain becomes the criterion
in exactly two places.

210
00:15:24.883 --> 00:15:28.875
The first is studs in a
flange, segments in a clamp,

211
00:15:28.915 --> 00:15:33.203
and locking segments in a
connector, when they are bent.

212
00:15:33.243 --> 00:15:38.515
For these parts, NORSOK does not wait
for the structure to become unstable.

213
00:15:38.555 --> 00:15:43.524
The yielding capacity is the load at which
the whole root cross section has reached

214
00:15:43.564 --> 00:15:47.434
0.2 % equivalent plastic strain.

215
00:15:47.474 --> 00:15:51.224
A stud that has yielded right
through is permanently stretched,

216
00:15:51.264 --> 00:15:53.894
and its preload is gone.

217
00:15:54.850 --> 00:15:57.139
The second is local failure.

218
00:15:57.179 --> 00:16:02.779
NORSOK defines it as a ductile crack that
starts at a point of high triaxial stress,

219
00:16:02.899 --> 00:16:06.355
as tiny voids form, grow, and join.

220
00:16:06.395 --> 00:16:11.321
Triaxiality is the mean stress
divided by the von Mises stress.

221
00:16:11.361 --> 00:16:14.412
In a tensile test it is one third.

222
00:16:14.452 --> 00:16:19.182
At a notch, where the material
is held back in every direction,

223
00:16:19.282 --> 00:16:21.299
it can be well above one.

224
00:16:21.339 --> 00:16:25.382
ASME Section VIII,
Division 3, clause KD-232,

225
00:16:25.422 --> 00:16:30.719
gives the strain limit as an
exponential function of triaxiality.

226
00:16:30.759 --> 00:16:36.641
At a triaxiality of 1.2, a
steel has only about 18 %

227
00:16:36.681 --> 00:16:40.023
of the ductility it
shows in a tensile test.

228
00:16:40.063 --> 00:16:44.943
The plastic strain is summed over the
load history, preload, pressure test,

229
00:16:44.983 --> 00:16:49.369
and the yielding capacity, and
the damage shall not exceed one.

230
00:16:49.648 --> 00:16:55.077
NORSOK sets the ASME load
factor for this check to 1.0,

231
00:16:55.117 --> 00:17:00.427
and runs the history all the
way up to the yielding capacity.

232
00:17:01.383 --> 00:17:04.083
In practice there is a shortcut.

233
00:17:04.123 --> 00:17:09.001
Carbon and low alloy steels that meet
the ductility requirements need no local

234
00:17:09.041 --> 00:17:15.475
failure check: an elongation of at least
17 % and a reduction of area of at least

235
00:17:15.515 --> 00:17:22.356
35 %, for a minimum yield
strength up to 655 MPa.

236
00:17:22.396 --> 00:17:26.263
Studs and nuts made to
annex D are exempt too.

237
00:17:26.303 --> 00:17:29.085
Our 75K forging meets this.

238
00:17:29.125 --> 00:17:32.795
In the elastic route, the
same idea is a simple sum:

239
00:17:32.835 --> 00:17:36.175
the three principal
stresses together shall stay

240
00:17:36.215 --> 00:17:40.046
below 2.5 times the
minimum yield strength.

241
00:17:41.283 --> 00:17:44.399
The remaining modes are load based again.

242
00:17:44.439 --> 00:17:49.335
The buckling capacity is the
maximum load divided by 1.10,

243
00:17:49.375 --> 00:17:56.085
the mechanical disengagement capacity is
the load at disengagement divided by 1.1,

244
00:17:56.125 --> 00:18:01.225
and ratcheting only needs
checking above 180 degrees.

245
00:18:01.265 --> 00:18:06.335
Functional capacities, like seal leak
tightness and hub face separation,

246
00:18:06.375 --> 00:18:11.735
use an Fd of one, with acceptance
criteria from the manufacturer,

247
00:18:11.775 --> 00:18:13.615
validated by testing.

248
00:18:17.750 --> 00:18:19.711
Back to the red spot.

249
00:18:19.751 --> 00:18:27.124
Our well is in 350 metres of water, with
the blowout preventer on the wellhead.

250
00:18:27.164 --> 00:18:32.239
Table A.4 gives the design
loads at the wellhead datum.

251
00:18:32.279 --> 00:18:40.800
The accidental case is 12 217
kNm of bending, 892 kN of shear,

252
00:18:40.840 --> 00:18:45.630
and 3424 kN of tension.

253
00:18:45.670 --> 00:18:54.629
The neck is 0.6 metres below the datum, so
the shear adds to the moment: 12.75 MNm.

254
00:18:55.583 --> 00:18:59.209
The well pressure also
pulls on the housing.

255
00:18:59.249 --> 00:19:02.276
Formula B.7 uses the true wall tension:

256
00:19:02.316 --> 00:19:08.176
the applied tension plus the pressure
end load on the gasket sealing diameter.

257
00:19:08.216 --> 00:19:16.126
With 690 bar on a sealing diameter of
half a metre, the end load is 13.5 MN,

258
00:19:16.236 --> 00:19:20.732
and the true wall tension becomes 17.0 MN.

259
00:19:21.683 --> 00:19:23.169
Now the check.

260
00:19:23.209 --> 00:19:28.929
The limit load analysis gave a
bending capacity of 20.3 MNm,

261
00:19:29.079 --> 00:19:35.630
and the tension capacity, σy
times the area, is 107 MN.

262
00:19:35.670 --> 00:19:46.020
For the accidental case: 17 over 107,
plus 12.75 over 20.3, divided by 0.9,

263
00:19:46.060 --> 00:19:48.150
gives 0.88.

264
00:19:48.190 --> 00:19:49.409
It passes.

265
00:19:49.449 --> 00:19:56.429
The extreme case, with a design
factor of 0.8, gives 0.61.

266
00:19:56.469 --> 00:20:01.039
The same neck failed the
elastic check by 4 %,

267
00:20:01.209 --> 00:20:05.219
because that check stops at the
first yield through the wall,

268
00:20:05.339 --> 00:20:08.689
and ignores the reserve
of the full section.

269
00:20:09.950 --> 00:20:14.263
To finish the job, we tick
off the other failure modes.

270
00:20:14.303 --> 00:20:19.787
Local failure: exempt, because the
forging meets the ductility requirements.

271
00:20:19.827 --> 00:20:23.192
Buckling: not an issue
for a wall this thick.

272
00:20:23.232 --> 00:20:25.832
The functional capacities
of the connector,

273
00:20:25.912 --> 00:20:30.852
like hub face separation and gasket
leak tightness at minimum preload,

274
00:20:30.972 --> 00:20:34.624
come from the supplier, and
connector capacities from

275
00:20:34.664 --> 00:20:39.039
FE analysis must be validated
by a full scale test.

276
00:20:39.079 --> 00:20:44.939
So, the verdict on the red
spot: 793 MPa of peak stress,

277
00:20:44.979 --> 00:20:47.726
and the wellhead is still acceptable.

278
00:20:52.183 --> 00:20:54.969
Let's put the answers on one page.

279
00:20:55.009 --> 00:20:57.777
Is the criterion the yield strength?

280
00:20:57.817 --> 00:21:00.939
The yield strength is the
anchor of every check,

281
00:21:00.979 --> 00:21:04.964
but it is never compared
with a peak stress.

282
00:21:05.004 --> 00:21:08.754
In the elastic route it
limits linearised stresses,

283
00:21:08.874 --> 00:21:11.597
and in the elastic plastic
route it shapes the

284
00:21:11.637 --> 00:21:14.728
material curve that produces the capacity.

285
00:21:14.768 --> 00:21:16.677
Is it a strain limit?

286
00:21:16.717 --> 00:21:18.190
Not for yielding.

287
00:21:18.230 --> 00:21:22.640
Strain is the criterion only for
studs and segments in bending,

288
00:21:22.780 --> 00:21:26.707
and for local failure, unless
the material is ductile enough.

289
00:21:26.747 --> 00:21:32.515
And from the FE model we extract linearised
stresses along classification lines,

290
00:21:32.555 --> 00:21:36.145
or a load rotation curve to instability.

291
00:21:36.185 --> 00:21:41.185
Whichever route you take, the
final check is always the same.

292
00:21:41.465 --> 00:21:46.183
The load shall not exceed
Fd times the capacity.

293
00:21:47.150 --> 00:21:51.936
The rules are in NORSOK U-001, Annex B.

294
00:21:51.976 --> 00:21:54.616
The details of the linearisation,

295
00:21:54.696 --> 00:21:59.493
the material curve and the strain
limit are in the ASME code.

296
00:21:59.533 --> 00:22:02.081
The worked example used
illustrative inputs,

297
00:22:02.121 --> 00:22:05.621
so take your design values
from your own project.

298
00:22:05.661 --> 00:22:07.302
Thanks for watching.

